<section class="pass" aria-labelledby="pass-title">
  <div class="pass-main">
    <p class="pass-kicker">Rendered in Cloudflare datacenter</p>
    <p class="flap" aria-label="<%= @runtime["colo"] %>">
      <% for letter, i in @runtime["colo"].upcase.chars %><span style="--i: <%= i %>" aria-hidden="true"><%= letter %></span><% end %>
    </p>
    <h1 id="pass-title">A Soli app, answered by the datacenter nearest you</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      No server, no container. The Soli interpreter, compiled to WebAssembly,
      ran this page's route, controller and view inside a Cloudflare Worker,
      a few milliseconds from where you are.
    </p>
    <p class="actions">
      <a class="button" href="/guide">Deploy your own</a>
      <a class="button ghost" href="/try">Try it here</a>
    </p>
  </div>
  <dl class="pass-stub">
    <div><dt>Visitor</dt><dd><%= @runtime["country"] %></dd></div>
    <div><dt>Datacenter</dt><dd><%= @runtime["colo"] %></dd></div>
    <div><dt>Runtime</dt><dd><%= @runtime["runtime"] %></dd></div>
    <div><dt>Soli</dt><dd><%= @runtime["version"] %></dd></div>
    <div><dt>Rendered (UTC)</dt><dd><%= @runtime["rendered_at"].substring(11, 19) %></dd></div>
  </dl>
</section>

<section class="board-section" aria-labelledby="board-title">
  <h2 id="board-title">What a Worker app can reach</h2>
  <p class="muted">The same models, jobs and attachments <code>soli serve</code> runs, wired to Cloudflare's own services.</p>
  <table class="board">
    <thead>
      <tr><th scope="col">Service</th><th scope="col">What your app does with it</th><th scope="col">Status</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td class="code"><a href="#sqlite">D1</a></td>
        <td>Models in SQLite, next to the Worker; a <code>grouped</code> block's reads in one <code>batch()</code></td>
        <td><a class="status live" href="/d1">Live on this site</a></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td class="code"><a href="#sqlite">Durable Objects</a></td>
        <td>A SQLite database per tenant, created the first time it is named</td>
        <td><span class="status">Soli 2.22</span></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td class="code"><a href="#postgres">Hyperdrive</a></td>
        <td>Your Postgres, with transactions and column-aware models</td>
        <td><span class="status">Soli 2.21</span></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td class="code"><a href="#r2">R2</a></td>
        <td>Uploads, and video that seeks: <code>Range</code>, <code>304</code></td>
        <td><span class="status">Soli 2.21</span></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td class="code"><a href="#images">Images</a></td>
        <td>Resize and convert on read: <code>photo.jpg?w=400&amp;fmt=webp</code></td>
        <td><span class="status">Soli 2.21</span></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td class="code"><a href="#queues">Queues</a></td>
        <td><code>perform_later</code>, retried with backoff</td>
        <td><span class="status">Soli 2.21</span></td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</section>

<section class="gates" aria-labelledby="gates-title">
  <h2 id="gates-title">Each service, wired</h2>
  <p class="muted">A binding in <code>wrangler.toml</code>, and the app's code stays as it is under <code>soli serve</code>.
     What each one keeps from you is listed with it.</p>

  <div class="gate-grid">
    <article class="gate" id="postgres">
      <header><span class="gate-code">PG</span><h3>Postgres, through Hyperdrive</h3></header>
      <p>Your existing database. Hyperdrive keeps the connections open next to it, so a request
         does not pay a TLS handshake to reach it.</p>
<pre><code class="language-toml">compatibility_flags = ["nodejs_compat"]

[[hyperdrive]]
binding = "HYPERDRIVE"
id = "…"   # npx wrangler hyperdrive create

[vars]
SOLI_DB_ADAPTER = "postgres"</code></pre>
      <ul>
        <li>The same SQL as <code>soli serve</code>: document tables, column-aware models
            (<code>table "orders"</code>), and <code>Model.transaction</code> — each request runs on one connection.</li>
        <li><code>soli edge build</code> bundles the <code>pg</code> driver, about 40 KB, when the app names Postgres.</li>
        <li>MySQL stays with <code>soli serve</code> for now: the Worker has no glue for its driver yet.</li>
      </ul>
    </article>

    <article class="gate" id="r2">
      <header><span class="gate-code">R2</span><h3>Uploads and video</h3></header>
      <p>A multipart form reaches the action as it does on a server; the file is kept in R2,
         through the binding — no keys in the app.</p>
<pre><code class="language-soli">class Photo &lt; Model
  has_one_attached("image", {"service": "r2", "max_size": 20_000_000})
end</code></pre>
<pre><code class="language-toml">[[r2_buckets]]
binding = "ATTACHMENTS"
bucket_name = "my-app-files"</code></pre>
      <ul>
        <li>Reads never cross wasm: the Worker answers from the bucket, <code>206</code> for a seek,
            <code>304</code> for a revalidation, at flat memory.</li>
        <li><code>soli serve</code> reaches the same objects through R2's S3 API, under the same keys.</li>
        <li>The body arrives whole: keep <code>max_size</code> near 20 MB. No resumable or direct uploads.</li>
      </ul>
    </article>

    <article class="gate" id="images">
      <header><span class="gate-code">IMG</span><h3>Images, transformed on read</h3></header>
      <p>The original is stored once; each URL asks for the size and format it needs.</p>
<pre><code class="language-toml">[images]
binding = "IMAGES"</code></pre>
<pre><code class="language-soli">photo.image_url({"w": 400, "fmt": "webp"})</code></pre>
      <ul>
        <li>Width, thumb, square, crop, rotate, grey, quality; WebP and AVIF out.</li>
        <li>Run on Cloudflare's machines, not in the Worker's CPU budget. Billed past the free monthly allowance.</li>
        <li>Without the binding, the URL answers the stored bytes.</li>
      </ul>
    </article>

    <article class="gate" id="queues">
      <header><span class="gate-code">JOB</span><h3>Background jobs on Queues</h3></header>
      <p><code>perform_later</code>, <code>perform_in</code> and <code>perform_at</code> send the job as a
         message; the same Worker runs it with the job classes from <code>app/jobs/</code>.</p>
<pre><code class="language-toml">[[queues.producers]]
binding = "JOBS"
queue = "my-app-jobs"

[[queues.consumers]]
queue = "my-app-jobs"
max_retries = 100</code></pre>
      <ul>
        <li>A failure is retried with the server's backoff (5 s, 10 s, 20 s…, an hour at most) until its <code>max_retries</code>.</li>
        <li>At least once: keep <code>perform</code> idempotent.</li>
        <li>Delays up to 12 hours. A <code>static cron</code> becomes a Cron Trigger: see the hourly job on <a href="/d1">/d1</a>.</li>
      </ul>
    </article>

    <article class="gate gate-wide" id="sqlite">
      <header><span class="gate-code">SQL</span><h3>SQLite: D1, or a Durable Object per tenant</h3></header>
      <p>D1 holds the app's tables next to the Worker. For one database per customer, a Durable Object
         with SQLite storage is created the first time its name is used — nothing to declare or
         redeploy when a site is added.</p>
      <div class="gate-pair">
<pre><code class="language-toml"># config/database.toml
[connections.primary]   # accounts, sites
adapter = "d1"
url = "d1://DB"

[connections.site]      # each site's own data
adapter = "d1"
url = "do://SITES/{tenant}"</code></pre>
<pre><code class="language-toml"># wrangler.toml
[[durable_objects.bindings]]
name = "SITES"
class_name = "SoliSqlObject"

[[migrations]]
tag = "v1"
new_sqlite_classes = ["SoliSqlObject"]</code></pre>
      </div>
      <ul>
        <li><code>switch_tenant(site.db_key)</code> in a middleware picks the database; <code>SoliSqlObject</code> ships in every Worker.</li>
        <li>A <code>grouped</code> block's reads go in one round trip: one D1 <code>batch()</code>, or one call to the object.</li>
        <li>No <code>Model.transaction</code> on either; a Durable Object holds up to 10 GB.</li>
      </ul>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="ship" aria-labelledby="ship-title">
  <h2 id="ship-title">From an app folder to a Worker</h2>
  <pre><code class="language-bash">./scripts/build-edge.sh                    # once per Soli version, in its source tree
soli new my_app --edge &amp;&amp; cd my_app       # wrangler.toml, D1, cookie sessions
soli edge dev                              # on workerd, rebuilt as you edit
soli edge build &amp;&amp; cd dist/edge &amp;&amp; npx wrangler deploy</code></pre>
  <p class="muted">An app you already have changes nothing but gaining a <code>wrangler.toml</code>; the <a href="/guide">guide</a> walks through it, and <a href="/limits">limits</a> lists what stays with <code>soli serve</code>.</p>
</section>

